From 08bf1787cc9aba6b3cfd68f04d8d1b3e05e9a72e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:12:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 17/26] windows: implement the host end of a guest-IPC vsock
 port over AF_UNIX

`krun_add_vsock_port2()` is called on every nerdbox boot -- port 1025 for ttrpc
to vminitd, port 1026 for stdio -- and had no Windows backend. Add one, and
build the vsock device on Windows.

A named pipe is the obvious-looking analogue of `unix.rs` and is the wrong
object, for two independent reasons.

The first is interoperability, and it settles it. libkrun owns only one end of
this connection. nerdbox creates the other in platform-neutral Go with no build
tags: `internal/vm/libkrun/instance.go` calls `net.Listen("unix", socketPath)`
for 1025 and `net.Dial("unix", streamPath)` for 1026, on ordinary filesystem
paths in the shim's state directory. Go implements those with Winsock AF_UNIX on
Windows. A `\\.\pipe\...` endpoint would never meet theirs. nerdbox does use
named pipes elsewhere -- `console_windows.go`, `io_copystreams_windows.go` -- so
the plain paths here are a deliberate distinction.

The second is that a pipe does not fit this device's I/O model. `process_event()`
is a readiness interface: it learns a handle is ready and then reads into a
virtqueue descriptor popped *after* waking. Windows named pipes have no
readiness state -- they are completion-based, so a read's destination must be
chosen before the wait, which would force a per-connection staging buffer and a
rewrite of the receive path. AF_UNIX keeps the readiness model, and the new
proxy is close to a syscall-for-syscall translation of `unix.rs`, with identical
credit accounting and status transitions.

Readiness reaches the epoll shim through `WSAEventSelect()`. A Winsock SOCKET is
not a waitable object whose signaled state means "readable", so it cannot be
registered with `utils::windows::epoll` directly -- that shim bridges handles
into an IOCP with `NtAssociateWaitCompletionPacket`, which reports a dispatcher
object becoming signaled. `WSAEventSelect()` provides exactly that: a Windows
Event, the object the shim was built for. So the registered handle, and what
`as_raw_fd()` returns, is the WSAEVENT and never the socket.

Two consequences shape the code and are documented at the top of the new module.
The shim's `wait()` echoes back the interest mask it was given rather than any
observed readiness, so `process_event()`'s `evset` argument carries no
information and the real state is recovered with `WSAEnumNetworkEvents()`. And
because that call, not the kernel, is what resets the event, every registration
is `EDGE_TRIGGERED` and every `process_event()` returns a `polling` update so
the muxer's delete-then-add re-arms the watch afterwards.

Winsock errors are translated to negative Linux errnos through the existing
`linux_errno_raw()`, since `WSAE*` codes are `10000 + BSD errno` and the guest
interprets the number.

Not executed. This compiles and links for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc -- krun.dll now
imports the 15 ws2_32 entry points it uses -- and has never run: there is no
Windows host here and no VM has been booted against it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
